        "authors_interpretation": "Authors propose that subtype-specific probability of forming a catalytically competent chromatin complex explains the activity difference. They interpret the transfer-count distribution as weak processivity and allow a second transfer on the same histone, same nucleosome or a neighboring nucleosome.",
        "curator_inference": {
          "text": "The active/inactive classification is operationally based on detected transfer. It supports a binding-versus-productivity distinction but does not directly image an active structural conformation.",
          "spatial_limit": "Fiber-level fluorescence does not identify the exact acceptor histone or nucleosome for each successive transfer. One or two steps must not be relabelled as a measured nearest-neighbor spreading range.",
          "waiting_time_limit": "Binding-to-first-transfer waiting contains E2 arrival, docking and chemistry. The paper’s estimated E2 association rate assumes E2~Ub recruitment is rate limiting; it is not a directly isolated chemical transfer constant."
        },
        "limits": [
          "All measurements are on reconstituted arrays, not mouse ESC chromatin or cellular RNA.",
          "PCGF4 is in a RYBP-containing variant trimer, not canonical PRC1.",
          "Do not equate all-binding τres, 2 with the conditioned productive-event τres, act. Binding and writing also differ in buffer MgCl2 and E2 loading.",
          "The H2AK118R/K119R control remains a poor rather than universally zero-reactivity substrate; other nearby lysines may be modified slowly.",
          "Preloaded E2 is used for writing, while the 50 nM E2 control in the separate binding experiment is unloaded.",
          "The ensemble PCGF4 NTD/K73R variants do not have measured single-molecule kon, residence or productive-event probabilities here."
        ],
